--TEST--
a metric label value refuses a tainted argument, and so does the series name — `rule:security/metric-label-refuses-tainted`, `rule:observability/metrics-three-members`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A user id, a tenant name or an error message in a label is unbounded
// cardinality, which is the one way a program can make a registry cost what the
// per-core bound exists to stop. What refuses it is the ordinary argument
// check rather than a qualifier rule: `array<tainted string>` is not the
// `array<string>` the bag declares. Asked of all three verbs, because a bag
// that lost the declaration on one of them would leave one spelling of the
// same mistake compiling.
tainted string $user = "u-1";

Core\Metrics::increment("logins_total", {labels: ["user" => $user]});
Core\Metrics::observe("login_seconds", 0.5, {labels: ["user" => $user]});
Core\Metrics::gauge("logins_in_flight", 1.0, {labels: ["user" => $user]});

// The name is a sink for the same reason and a stronger one: a label value
// multiplies a series, while a name *is* one.
Core\Metrics::increment($user);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:11:61
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:12:65
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:13:66
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:17:25
%A
